  "body": "## Request\n\nThe maintainer asked for this in chat on 2026-11-23. His words: \"Aur package; should rebuild the Linux build that comes before it and not rebuild it again from scratch.. Waste of time and action credits.\" He set the priority P2.\n\n## Problem\n\nIn `release.yml`, the AUR package step compiles CamCraft again from the source. The `linux` job of the same run has already built the same release binaries from the same tag. The second build costs time and billed GitHub Actions minutes (AGENTS.md: the minutes are budgeted).\n\n## Expected\n\n- The AUR package uses the Linux release binaries that the `linux` job of the same run made. Use an artifact (`actions/upload-artifact` in the `linux` job, `actions/download-artifact` in the AUR step), or the release asset of the same tag.\n- The AUR package does not run `cargo build` again.\n- The PKGBUILD that we publish stays correct for AUR users. If the AUR rules need a from-source PKGBUILD, publish it unchanged, and only the CI check of the package uses the prebuilt binaries. Record which way was chosen, and why, in `docs/decisions/`.\n- The packaged binary is byte-identical to the binary in the Linux release asset. A check compares the checksums.\n\n## Notes\n\n- This changes a file in `.github/workflows/`. Use the `runners` skill. Add no trigger. The maintainer ordered this change.\n- Measure the minutes of a tag run before and after, and put the numbers in the PR body.\n- Test the change with a `workflow_dispatch` dry run, not with a new tag.\n",
